{
  "abstract": "Equal values in an input embedding and output head do not establish shared trainable parameters. We audit the Qwen3-0.6B-Base checkpoint and batched loading path supplied for r/math_rlvr. Although the official configuration specifies tied embeddings and the converted base arrays are identical, the supplied implementation creates independent Parameters: 751,632,384 unique trainable scalars instead of 596,049,920, a 26.1% increase. After 50 ordinary RLVR updates, the arrays already differ, so silently tying the learned checkpoint would be a new intervention. We provide explicit construction, optimizer, serialization and restoration checks, then run two prespecified tied-from-base controls with the supplied 50-update objective. On a fixed 224-question validation set, tying changes accuracy from 98 to 109 correct for seed 17041 and from 108 to 99 for seed 27041. Paired item-bootstrap intervals include zero in both cases and do not estimate seed uncertainty. The homogeneous-group execution choice is matched within each seed block but differs across blocks. Tied training reduces observed peak allocation by about 1.16 GiB; it does not establish a capability or runtime advantage. The contribution is a reproducible correction to the experimental parameterization and a bounded counterfactual, not a novel tying method. We release all 896 raw validation completions, compact training evidence, source and CPU checks. Researchers should report this supplied path as untied and treat restoration of sharing as a changed training graph.",
